            <ArticleTitle>Oral dexketoprofen for pain treatment during diagnostic hysteroscopy in postmenopausal women.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>277-81</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the efficacy of dexketoprofen (DEX) in reducing pain at different stages of the hysteroscopic procedure in comparison with local anaesthesia in menopausal women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Menopausal patients affected by uterine bleeding submitted to diagnostic hysteroscopy, were randomised to receive either 25 mg DEX tablet (n = 148) or intracervical injection of 5 ml mepivacaine 2% (n = 150). Pain suffered during the procedure itself and 30, 60, 120 min after, was scored on the 11 point Visual Analogic Scale, recorded and analysed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">No statistical difference were noted during the procedure itself in both groups of treatment. Patients treated with DEX has significantly less postoperative pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">DEX is not superior to mepivacaine in reducing the discomfort of the procedure but does significantly reduce postoperative pain.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright 2002 Elsevier Science Ireland Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
